1159: \begin{theorem}[Finite-coordinate determination and trust-boundary classifier]
1160: \label{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}
1161: Fix the dispatch table $\Gamma$ and the verifier $V$.  Let $F_{\Gamma}$ be the
1162: finite schema projection read by $V$ from a certificate record, namely the
1163: claim-kind set, declared evidence level, evidence surface, promotion field,
1164: gate-pass rows, discovery-source rows, command-boundary tuple, and the assigned
1165: gate set determined by $\Gamma$.  If two ambient pipeline or artifact states
1166: induce the same values of all coordinates in $F_{\Gamma}$ for a claim $c$, then
1167: they induce the same record-acceptance decision and the same finite publication
1168: certificate for $c$.  Therefore the exactness of
1169: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} is exactness only relative to this
1170: finite schema projection.  No property depending on a coordinate outside
1171: $F_{\Gamma}$---for example the semantic truth of a referenced theorem, truthful
1172: production or implementation soundness of a gate row, a fresh Lean build result,
1173: a daemon trace, an artifact semantic fact, a theorem-environment completeness
1174: claim, or an external venue decision---is a consequence of the accepted
1175: certificate unless that property is first encoded as an additional finite
1176: certificate coordinate and the enlarged record is accepted.  In particular,
1177: record-gate soundness is a load-bearing hypothesis for
1178: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}; it is not supplied by schema
1179: exactness alone.
1180: \end{theorem}
1181: 
1182: \begin{proof}
1183: The verifier $V$ is defined as an ordered list of six checks, and each check reads
1184: only one of the coordinates listed in $F_{\Gamma}$ together with the fixed finite
1185: dispatch table $\Gamma$.  If two ambient states induce the same values on
1186: $F_{\Gamma}$, then the input tuple \((K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\), the assigned gate set,
1187: and every submitted pointer queried by $V$ are identical in the two states.  Each
1188: of the six checks therefore has the same truth value in the two states, so $V$
1189: either accepts both records or rejects both records at the same first failed
1190: finite field.  By Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, an accepted
1191: decision is equivalent to the same six-clause record-acceptance semantics, hence
1192: the finite certificate determined by those clauses is also the same.  This proves
1193: the schema-relative and finite-record-relative factorisation.
1194: 
1195: Now let $Q$ be a property whose truth can vary while all coordinates in
1196: $F_{\Gamma}$ are held fixed.  Then there exist two ambient states with the same
1197: certificate tuple and the same verifier decision but different truth values for
1198: $Q$.  A conclusion whose truth changes under such an $F_{\Gamma}$-preserving
1199: substitution is not determined by the accepted certificate.  To make $Q$
1200: load-bearing, the operational interface must add a finite coordinate recording
1201: the required evidence: for a build or daemon trace this is a command-run tuple;
1202: for an artifact semantic fact this is an artifact pass record or fresh evaluator
1203: rerun log; for theorem-environment completeness this is an extractor command,
1204: source state, environment, exit code, and log path; and for a venue decision this
1205: is an external evidence surface and the corresponding route/submission record.
1206: Without that enlargement, Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} supplies
1207: only the original finite-record conclusion.
1208: 
1209: Finally, the same argument isolates record-gate soundness.  The predicate
1210: $\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)$ says that recorded ledger entries actually contain
1211: the assigned passing rows and submitted evidence pointers.  A package can agree
1212: on the schema shape of a candidate certificate while differing on whether such a
1213: record-gate-sound ledger has been supplied.  Thus schema exactness checks the
1214: finite acceptance relation after the row data are present; it does not by itself
1215: make those rows available, truthfully produced, or semantically adequate.
1216: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} therefore states record-gate
1217: soundness as an explicit hypothesis, and the trust boundary is exactly the finite
1218: projection $F_{\Gamma}$ plus that hypothesis.
1219: \end{proof}
1220: 
1221: \begin{proposition}[Machine-readable certificate records]
1222: \label{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records}
1223: For the current presentation package, the finite record checker
1224: $V$ of Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier}, with the
1225: record-acceptance semantics of
1226: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, has an executable record-level
1227: instance over the review bundle, conditional on exact supplemental inclusion of
1228: \path{review_bundle/} in the completed submission with matching final digests, as stated in
1229: Proposition~\ref{audit:review-bundle-availability-condition}.  The schema file
1230: \path{review_bundle/certificate_schema.json}, the pass-record ledger
1231: \path{review_bundle/current_package_pass_records.json}, the primary-interface map
1232: \path{review_bundle/submission_interface_map.json}, the primary-claim inventory
1233: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory.json}, the verifier script
1234: \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py}, and the recorded run
1235: \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log} instantiate the verifier at
1236: the declared evidence levels for entries $a_1,\ldots,a_7$.  The negative fixture
1237: \path{review_bundle/negative_certificate_record_example.json} and recorded run
1238: \path{review_bundle/negative_certificate_record_example_run.md} instantiate the first
1239: failure mode for a forbidden command-run escalation.  Each pass record in the
1240: ledger has the five fields \emph{gate name}, \emph{input surface},
1241: \emph{decision}, \emph{evidence pointer}, and \emph{evidence level}.  The
1242: machine-readable submission-interface map is locked to rows \path{sim1}--\path{sim4}
1243: of Table~\ref{tab:submission-interface-map} by its row identifiers, certificates,
1244: surfaces, and evidence levels; the primary-claim inventory is locked to rows
1245: \path{pc1}--\path{pc12} by its row identifiers, source locations, row classes,
1246: and evidence levels.  These executable locks check the finite record interface;
1247: full semantic equality between prose paraphrases and mathematical meaning,
1248: soundness of the gate implementations, and path completeness beyond the declared
1249: surfaces remain outside the record-check claim and stay at the manual audit
1250: boundary stated in the row classes.
1251: \end{proposition}
1252: 
1253: \begin{proof}
1254: All objects in the statement are finite files in the submitted review bundle, so
1255: the claim is a finite record check.  The manifest
1256: \path{review_bundle/REVIEW_BUNDLE_MANIFEST.json} contains top-level fields named
1257: \path{certificate_schema}, \path{current_package_pass_records},
1258: \path{submission_interface_map}, \path{primary_claim_inventory},
1259: \path{verifier_script}, and \path{verifier_run_log}; these fields point to the verifier
1260: bundle files named in the statement.  Thus the executable verifier is not an
1261: implicit prose convention but a submitted artifact surface.
1262: 
1263: The schema file lists the seven claim kinds, the two evidence levels, the same
1264: gate dispatch $\Gamma$ used in the supplement, and the required pass-record
1265: fields $(g,I,d,e,\ell)$.  The pass-record ledger has exactly seven entries,
1266: $a_1,\ldots,a_7$.  For each entry it records a nonempty claim-kind set, the
1267: path-verified evidence level, a finite evidence surface, the route-boundary or
1268: human-promotion field, pass records, the discovery-interface field, and the
1269: evidence boundary.  Inspecting the seven entries gives the required gate coverage:
1270: $a_1$ has inventory and marker-existence records; $a_2$ has promotion, overlap,
1271: submission-state, and submission-pack records; $a_3$ has promotion, overlap, and
1272: submission-state records; $a_4$ has promotion, overlap, submission-state, and
1273: submission-pack records; $a_5$ has pinned-discovery and theorem-content-delta
1274: records; and $a_6$ has status and limitation-ledger records.  These are exactly
1275: the unions of the gate sets assigned by $\Gamma$ to the claim-kind sets displayed
1276: in Table~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger}.  The only row with discovery kind
1277: is $a_5$, and it is the only row with a non-null discovery-interface field; every
1278: path-verified row carries a boundary saying that no command-run assertion is
1279: made.  The seventh row $a_7$ is the bibliography/venue-freshness traceability and
1280: submission-pack record; it is path-verified in the acceptance ledger, while the
1281: separate command-boundary inventory below records the live-check command log used
1282: for the bounded venue and bibliography freshness assertion.
1283: 
1284: The script \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py} implements precisely
1285: these finite checks: required manifest fields, entries $a_1$--$a_7$, kind-domain
1286: membership, evidence-level membership, assigned-gate coverage, the five
1287: pass-record fields, evidence-level agreement, discovery-field discipline, the
1288: path-verified boundary condition, exact row locks for \path{sim1}--\path{sim4}
1289: against the submission-interface JSON fields, and row-class locks for
1290: \path{pc1}--\path{pc12} against the primary-claim inventory locations, statuses,
1291: and evidence levels.  The recorded log
1292: \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log} records a successful run of
1293: that script over the submitted files and states the boundary that no Lean build,
1294: publication-daemon run, or Rule110 dynamic artifact rerun is being certified.
1295: The negative fixture log
1296: \path{review_bundle/negative_certificate_record_example_run.md} records the command
1297: \path{python review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py --negative-example review_bundle/negative_certificate_record_example.json}
1298: and the expected rejection of a route row whose evidence level is raised to
1299: command-run without the required command-run tuple.
1300: Consequently the executable artifact proves exactly the schema-level instance of
1301: $V$ claimed here.  By the finite-record exactness of Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, this is also
1302: an instance of the paper's finite record-acceptance semantics: the executable
1303: record accepts exactly the current-package certificates and rejects the
1304: recorded command-run escalation example; hence it supplies no stronger evidence
1305: level or omitted gate semantics.
1306: 
1307: Finally, \path{review_bundle/submission_interface_map.json} has rows
1308: \path{sim1}--\path{sim4}.  The verifier now rejects a mutation of any row's
1309: primary claim text, certificate list, row-or-manifest surface list, or evidence
1310: level, so the JSON file is an executable finite mirror of the interface table at
1311: that declared field level.  The file
1312: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory.json} lists the concrete primary-note
1313: assertions as rows \path{pc1}--\path{pc12}; every load-bearing row names a
1314: certificate, row, manifest surface, or bounded-context status and declares an
1315: evidence level, and the verifier locks each row's identifier, source location,
1316: status class, and evidence level.  Thus the primary note has a complete finite
1317: claim inventory at the declared review fields, and no additional primary
1318: load-bearing assertion is licensed by the machine-readable interface.  The proof
1319: does not assert that the script decides semantic paraphrase equality or discovers
1320: undeclared paths; those remain the manual audit obligations represented by the
1321: table rows and by Proposition~\ref{prop:primary-inventory-freshness-criterion}.\end{proof}
1322: 
1323: \begin{theorem}[Executable record non-escalation]
1324: \label{thm:executable-record-non-escalation}
1325: Let $F$ be the finite field projection read by
1326: \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py}: manifest-field presence,
1327: entries $a_1$--$a_6$, claim-kind membership, evidence-level membership,
1328: assigned-gate coverage, pass-record fields, evidence-level agreement,
1329: discovery-field discipline, path-verified boundary status, row locks for
1330: \path{sim1}--\path{sim4}, and row-class locks for \path{pc1}--\path{pc12}.  Assuming
1331: the exact-or-archive-equivalent review-bundle support surface of
1332: Proposition~\ref{audit:review-bundle-availability-condition}, among all
1333: assertions about the current review bundle that are functions only of this
1334: projection, Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records} proves the
1335: maximal executable claim: the six current-package certificates satisfy the finite
1336: record-acceptance relation of Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} at the
1337: declared path-verified fields under the input--predicate--output contract of
1338: Theorem~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract}, and no accepted row carries a
1339: command-run boundary.  Thus the bundled Python script is an implementation
1340: witness for the finite schema, not a separately formalized verifier-language
1341: semantics.  In particular, the executable verifier cannot certify any strictly
1342: stronger assertion whose truth depends on prose-to-semantics equality,
1343: independent extraction of claims from the manuscript, completeness of paths not
1344: declared in the records, or the external semantics of a Lean development,
1345: publication daemon, artifact suite, evaluator, or venue state.
1346: \end{theorem}
1347: 
1348: \begin{proof}
1349: By the proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records}, the
1350: script reads exactly the finite projection $F$ listed in the statement and rejects
1351: mutations of the locked fields.  Hence every executable assertion made by that
1352: script is constant on the equivalence classes of review bundles with the same
1353: projection $F$.  On this quotient, the six ledger entries, their kind sets, their
1354: evidence levels, their assigned gates, their pass-record fields, their discovery
1355: boundaries, and their path-verified non-command boundaries are all fixed.  By
1356: Theorem~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract}, the executable checker has no
1357: other mathematical output channel.  The finite-record acceptance exactness
1358: theorem then identifies precisely what this fixed data entails: acceptance of
1359: those six certificates in the finite relation
1360: $\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}$ and rejection of stronger readings that add kinds, raise
1361: the evidence level, omit route or discovery fields, omit pass records, or assert
1362: command-run evidence without the required command tuple.
1363: 
1364: Now consider any proposed stronger assertion of one of the four types named in
1365: the statement.  Its truth is not determined by $F$.  Prose-to-semantics equality
1366: depends on interpreting manuscript language against mathematical meanings, while
1367: $F$ contains only row identifiers, selected text locks, classes, locations,
1368: certificate lists, surfaces, and evidence levels.  Independent extraction depends
1369: on an extraction procedure over the manuscript, while $F$ contains the submitted
1370: inventory rather than the result of an independently specified extractor.
1371: Completeness of undeclared paths depends on paths outside the finite surfaces
1372: read by the verifier.  External artifact semantics depends on running or
1373: semantically validating objects whose command, source state, environment, exit
1374: code, and log path are not present in the path-verified rows.  Therefore two
1375: packages can agree on $F$ while differing on such a stronger assertion.  Since a
1376: function of $F$ has the same value on both packages, no executable claim proved by
1377: this verifier can certify that stronger assertion.  The maximal executable
1378: record-level claim is exactly the one stated in
1379: Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records}.\end{proof}
1380: 
1381: \begin{theorem}[Executable certificate-construction invariant]
1382: \label{thm:executable-certificate-construction-invariant}
1383: For the submitted review bundle, let $G$ be the union of the finite fields read by
1384: the two executable verifiers
1385: \path{review_bundle/verify_source_interface_record.py} and
1386: \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py}.  Thus $G$ contains the ten
1387: source-interface table triples, the JSON source-interface records, the pinned
1388: source snapshot path, digest, line count, declared source commit and line ranges,
1389: the six current-package pass-record entries $a_1,\ldots,a_6$, the submission-map
1390: rows \path{sim1}--\path{sim4}, the primary-inventory row locks
1391: \path{pc1}--\path{pc12}, and the two recorded successful verifier logs.  On the
1392: equivalence class of packages with the same projection $G$, the publication-safety
1393: interface has a unique executable finite certificate construction: the imported
1394: discovery-gate interface is the sixteen-row checked source interface of
1395: Proposition~\ref{prop:source-interface-record-file-consistency}, the accepted
1396: current-package certificates are exactly the six records of
1397: Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records}, and any stronger
1398: reading that changes the source interface, adds an accepted row, raises the
1399: evidence level, adds a command-run assertion, or asserts external semantic or
1400: venue correctness is not determined by $G$ and is rejected at the finite-record
1401: boundary unless new certificate fields and verifier records are supplied.  This
1402: is a finite-record construction invariant: the scripts witness the listed
1403: predicate checks on bundled JSON and table fields, and no claim is made here that
1404: Python itself has been given a separate formal operational semantics.
1405: \end{theorem}
1406: 
1407: \begin{proof}
1408: The first executable verifier reads only the manuscript source-interface table,
1409: \path{source_interface_record.json}, the \path{pinned_source_snapshot} object of
1410: \path{review_bundle/REVIEW_BUNDLE_MANIFEST.json}, and the bundled source snapshot.
1411: Proposition~\ref{prop:source-interface-record-file-consistency} proves that these
1412: fields agree on the ordered sixteen triples, the original path, the source commit, the
1413: sixteen line ranges, the snapshot digest, the snapshot line count, and the containment
1414: of each imported label in its declared range.  Hence every package with the same
1415: projection $G$ has the same executable imported discovery-gate interface, and no
1416: table-only, JSON-only, manifest-only, stale-digest, stale-line-count, or
1417: out-of-range imported row can be accepted inside that projection.
1418: 
1419: The second executable verifier reads the manifest fields, the certificate schema,
1420: the six pass-record entries, the submission-interface map, and the primary-claim
1421: inventory.  Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records} proves
1422: that these fields instantiate the finite verifier $V$ for exactly rows
1423: $a_1,\ldots,a_6$ at the path-verified level, with the displayed gate coverage,
1424: discovery-field discipline, row locks, and non-command boundary.  Therefore every
1425: package with the same projection $G$ has the same six accepted current-package
1426: certificates and the same absence of command-run evidence in those certificates.
1427: The input--predicate--output contract,
1428: Theorem~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract}, says that these are the only
1429: positive outputs available to the certificate checker; the finite-record
1430: exactness theorem,
1431: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, identifies these accepted records
1432: with the record-acceptance relation and rejects stronger readings that require
1433: additional claim kinds, evidence surfaces, promotion fields, discovery fields,
1434: pass rows, or command-run tuples.
1435: 
1436: Combining the two paragraphs gives the stated uniqueness on the $G$-projection:
1437: the discovery component of any accepted current-package claim is forced through
1438: the same checked ten-row source interface, and the publication component is forced
1439: through the same six checked certificate rows.  Any proposed stronger assertion
1440: listed in the statement either changes one of the finite fields of $G$, in which
1441: case it is not a package in the same equivalence class, or leaves $G$ unchanged,
1442: in which case the two verifiers receive identical inputs and can return only the
1443: same finite-record conclusions.  Such an unchanged projection contains no
1444: coordinate for external theorem truth, proof-assistant correctness, unlogged
1445: daemon execution, artifact semantic validity, or venue acceptance.  Those claims
1446: therefore require an enlarged certificate and additional executable records, and
1447: without them they are outside the accepted finite-record interface.\end{proof}
1448: 
1449: \begin{proposition}[Deterministic claim-kind dispatch]
1450: \label{prop:claim-kind-dispatch}
1451: For every load-bearing manuscript claim whose kind lies in the seven-kind domain
1452: above, $\Gamma$ assigns a unique finite set of deterministic gates.  In a
1453: pipeline that is record-gate-sound for acceptance, a mixed load-bearing claim may be
1454: used as accepted work only if its acceptance entry records passage through every
1455: gate in the union assigned to its component kinds.
1456: \end{proposition}
1457: 
1458: \begin{proof}
1459: The seven atomic clauses in the definition of $\Gamma$ assign one explicitly named
1460: finite gate set to each atomic claim kind.  Hence an atomic claim has a unique
1461: assigned gate set.  For a mixed claim, the component-kind set is finite because
1462: it is a subset of the seven-kind domain; taking the union of the corresponding
1463: finite gate sets is deterministic and finite.  Record-gate-soundness for acceptance
1464: requires the acceptance entry of a load-bearing claim to record passage through
1465: every gate assigned by $\Gamma$.  If a component gate of a mixed claim were
1466: omitted, then the projection of the claim to that component kind would lack its
1467: assigned pass record.  Such an entry would not be record-gate-sound.  Therefore accepted
1468: mixed claims in a record-gate-sound pipeline must pass every gate in the union.\end{proof}
1469: 
1470: \begin{theorem}[Mixed-claim projection obstruction]
1471: \label{thm:mixed-claim-projection-obstruction}
1472: Let $c$ be a load-bearing mixed claim with nonempty component-kind set
1473: $K\subseteq\mathsf{Dom}(\Gamma)$ and submitted candidate certificate
1474: $\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)$.  For a nonempty subset $J\subseteq K$, write
1475: \[
1476: G_J=\bigcup_{k\in J}\Gamma(k)
1477: \]
1478: and let $c_J$ denote the projected reading of $c$ that keeps exactly the
1479: claim-kind components in $J$ and keeps the same declared evidence level,
1480: evidence surface, promotion boundary, and command boundary, except that the
1481: discovery field is required precisely when discovery lies in $J$.  Then the
1482: following are equivalent for the projected reading $c_J$:
1483: \begin{enumerate}
1484: \item the finite verifier accepts the projected certificate
1485: $\mathcal C_J=(J,\ell,E,H,R|_{G_J},D_J,B)$, where $R|_{G_J}$ is the restriction of
1486: $R$ to rows whose gate name lies in $G_J$ and $D_J$ is $D$ exactly when discovery
1487: lies in $J$ and is absent otherwise;
1488: \item every gate $g\in G_J$ has in $R$ a pass record with the five required
1489: fields and with an allowed evidence pointer, the retained boundary fields $E$,
1490: $H$, $D_J$, and $B$ satisfy the corresponding schema clauses at level $\ell$, and
1491: $J\subseteq\mathsf{Dom}(\Gamma)$.
1492: \end{enumerate}
1493: Consequently, if an acceptance entry for $c$ records passage only through a
1494: proper gate subset $Q\subset\bigcup_{k\in K}\Gamma(k)$, then every component
1495: subset $J$ with $\neg(G_J\subseteq Q)$ has an inclusion-minimal finite bad subrecord,
1496: namely a missing pass record for some gate in $G_J\setminus Q$.  In particular,
1497: no passage through route, submission, scheduler, artifact, traceability,
1498: formal-source, or discovery gates can certify a mixed claim's remaining component
1499: unless the pass rows assigned to that remaining component are also present.
1500: \end{theorem}
1501: 
1502: \begin{proof}
1503: The projected certificate $\mathcal C_J$ is a certificate of the same schema as
1504: Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table}, with claim-kind set
1505: $J$ in place of $K$ and with gate field restricted to the finite set $G_J$.
1506: Because $J\subseteq K\subseteq\mathsf{Dom}(\Gamma)$, Proposition~\ref{prop:claim-kind-dispatch}
1507: assigns exactly the finite gate set $G_J$ to the projected reading.  Applying the
1508: schema-relative exactness theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness},
1509: to $\mathcal C_J$ gives the equivalence: acceptance is exactly satisfaction of
1510: the six finite schema clauses for the retained fields.  Clause A1 is the domain
1511: condition $J\subseteq\mathsf{Dom}(\Gamma)$ and $\ell\in\mathsf{Lev}$; clause A2
1512: checks the evidence surface $E$; clause A3 checks that each gate in $G_J$ has a
1513: pass record in $R|_{G_J}$ with an allowed pointer; clause A4 checks the retained
1514: promotion boundary $H$; clause A5 checks precisely the retained discovery field
1515: $D_J$; and clause A6 checks the retained command boundary $B$.  These are exactly
1516: the conditions in item~2, so items~1 and~2 are equivalent.
1517: 
1518: Now suppose the row for the mixed claim records only pass rows with gate names in
1519: a proper subset $Q$ of the full assigned gate set.  Choose a nonempty
1520: $J\subseteq K$ with $\neg(G_J\subseteq Q)$ and choose $g\in G_J\setminus Q$.  The
1521: projected verifier for $c_J$ must check $g$ by clause A3, because $g$ is assigned
1522: to the retained component-kind set $J$.  No row for $g$ is present among the
1523: recorded gate names in $Q$, so clause A3 fails.  The verifier reads only the
1524: finite fields consisting of $J$, $\Gamma$, the absent gate name $g$, and the gate
1525: row field $R$; deleting fields not read by this failed check leaves an
1526: inclusion-minimal bad subrecord of the second obstruction type in
1527: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}.  Since the argument
1528: uses only membership in the assigned gate set, it applies uniformly to each of
1529: the seven atomic gate interfaces.  Thus one component's gate passage cannot be
1530: substituted for another component's missing assigned pass rows in a mixed
1531: load-bearing claim.\end{proof}
1532: 
1533: \begin{theorem}[Assigned-gate soundness audit boundary]
1534: \label{thm:assigned-gate-soundness-audit-boundary}
1535: Let $P$ be a finite submitted package with pass-record ledger $L$, dispatch
1536: table $\Gamma$, and submitted support surface $S$.  For a load-bearing claim
1537: $c$, write $G(c)=\bigcup_{k\in K(c)}\Gamma(k)$ for the finite set of gates
1538: assigned to its claim-kind set.  The package may use a ledger row for $c$ as a
1539: sound record-gate witness only through the finite predicate
1540: $\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)$ of Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface}.
1541: Equivalently, the available finite-record strength is exactly the following:
1542: \begin{enumerate}
1543: \item for every $g\in G(c)$ the submitted ledger contains a pass row for $g$,
1544: including the gate name, input surface, decision, evidence pointer, and evidence
1545: level;
1546: \item every evidence pointer named by those pass rows denotes a submitted path,
1547: manifest row, theorem/proposition/table label, uploaded bundle file, or explicitly
1548: declared historical boundary at the evidence level stated in the row; and
1549: \item if the manuscript asserts any stronger proposition that the underlying gate
1550: implementation was independently sound, freshly executed, or semantically correct
1551: beyond the recorded finite schema check, the submitted package also contains a
1552: gate-soundness audit tuple for that stronger proposition: source path, command,
1553: source state, environment, exit code, and log path for the assigned gate.
1554: \end{enumerate}
1555: Consequently, in the absence of the audit tuple in (3), record-gate soundness is
1556: an explicit hypothesis of the publication-safety theorem, not a theorem proved by
1557: the current package.  The finite pass rows prove only the package-level statement
1558: that no accepted ledger claim omits an assigned gate row or its declared
1559: submitted evidence surface.
1560: \end{theorem}
1561: 
1562: \begin{proof}
1563: The dispatch table has finite values by Proposition~\ref{prop:claim-kind-dispatch};
1564: hence $G(c)$ is finite.  A submitted pass-record ledger and support surface are
1565: also finite.  The predicate $\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)$ therefore expands to a
1566: finite conjunction of row and pointer tests: for each accepted load-bearing claim
1567: it supplies a certificate satisfying $V(c,\mathcal C)=1$, and for each gate name
1568: in $G(c)$ it searches the rows attached to $c$ and compares the finite fields
1569: gate name, input surface, decision, evidence pointer, and evidence level.  If
1570: one of these rows is missing, the certificate contains the second bad subrecord
1571: of Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}, namely a missing
1572: pass record for an assigned gate.  If a row is present, the verifier has only the
1573: finite string and path fields carried by that row.
1574: 
1575: The second condition is exactly the local-checkability condition for the row's
1576: evidence pointer.  A pointer to a path, manifest row, theorem/proposition/table
1577: label, uploaded bundle file, or explicit historical boundary is a coordinate of
1578: the submitted finite record surface.  If the pointer does not denote one of
1579: those finite surfaces at the stated evidence level, the certificate has the first
1580: bad subrecord of Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}, a
1581: missing evidence surface.  Thus conditions (1) and (2) are precisely the
1582: submitted-row and submitted-pointer clauses of $\mathsf{RGS}_{S}$, and they are
1583: precisely what the package can verify from $L$ and the submitted surfaces.
1584: 
1585: The third condition concerns a different claim kind.  External implementation
1586: soundness, fresh execution, and semantic correctness assert facts about the
1587: operation of a gate outside the row's finite fields.  By the evidence-level
1588: definitions and by Theorem~\ref{thm:executable-record-non-escalation}, a
1589: command-result claim is not supplied by a path-verified pass row; it requires a
1590: command tuple with source state, environment, exit code, and log path.  By
1591: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, two worlds that have
1592: identical submitted pass rows but different unsubmitted gate implementations are
1593: indistinguishable to the finite verifier.  Hence no proof from (1) and (2) can
1594: establish the stronger external-gate proposition.  The stronger proposition is
1595: accepted only after the package is enlarged by the audit tuple in (3).  Without
1596: that enlargement, record-gate soundness remains the explicit finite predicate
1597: $\mathsf{RGS}_{S}$, while the proved package fact is exactly the absence of
1598: missing assigned-gate rows and missing declared evidence surfaces.\end{proof}
1599: 
1600: \begin{theorem}[Finite certificate obstruction skeleton]
1601: \label{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}
1602: Let $c$ be a load-bearing manuscript claim whose component kinds lie in the
1603: seven-kind domain.  The verifier of
1604: Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier} gives the following
1605: complete audit classification at the declared evidence level.  A certificate for
1606: $c$ passes the publication-safety interface if and only if it has no bad
1607: subrecord of the following five types:
1608: \begin{enumerate}
1609: \item missing evidence surface for the declared evidence level;
1610: \item missing pass record for a gate in $\bigcup_{k\in K}\Gamma(k)$;
1611: \item missing human-promotion record when the claim crosses from intake or
1612: source material into active publication work;
1613: \item discovery escalation without the pinned discovery-gate source-interface
1614: rows for certificate, ledger, lineage, and boundary witnesses; or
1615: \item command-run escalation without command, source commit, environment, exit
1616: code, and log path.
1617: \end{enumerate}
1618: Equivalently, every unsafe accepted claim contains an inclusion-minimal finite
1619: bad subrecord of one of these five types, and deleting all other fields preserves
1620: the same rejection by the verifier.  This is an audit-skeleton theorem for the
1621: software-workshop route: it classifies finite certificate failures and makes no
1622: claim about theorem-proving complexity or about fresh execution of the
1623: underlying workflow.
1624: \end{theorem}
1625: 
1626: \begin{proof}
1627: Let $K$ be the finite set of component kinds of $c$.  Since $K$ is a subset of
1628: the seven-kind domain, Proposition~\ref{prop:claim-kind-dispatch} gives a unique
1629: finite required gate set
1630: \[
1631: G(c)=\bigcup_{k\in K}\Gamma(k).
1632: \]
1633: The certificate field $R$ is indexed by exactly this finite set, so checking
1634: gate passage is a finite lookup: each $g\in G(c)$ must have a pass record and the
1635: record must cite an item of the finite evidence surface $E$.  The evidence-level
1636: field $\ell$ and boundary field $B$ make the same lookup finite for evidence
1637: strength: at path-verified level the certificate must name the source, artifact,
1638: pinned commit, local publication path, or gate-decision surface used by the
1639: claim; at command-run level it must additionally name command, source commit,
1640: environment, exit code, and log path.  Neither lookup interprets artifact
1641: semantics, reruns Lean, or predicts editorial outcomes; those are stronger claim
1642: kinds or stronger evidence levels that require their own records.  The human-promotion field $H$ separates
1643: the route boundary from all other gates: it is either a concrete promotion
1644: record or a finite marker that no such boundary is crossed.  Finally, when
1645: discovery lies in $K$, the discovery field $D$ names the pinned source-interface
1646: rows certified by Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness} and
1647: Propositions~\ref{prop:reviewable-source-snapshot-interface} and
1648: \ref{prop:source-interface-record-file-consistency}; these rows are finite and
1649: therefore locally checkable in the same way as the other certificate fields.
1650: Thus the certificate is sufficient for a reviewer to check safety by examining a
1651: finite list of fields and cited artifacts, not by trusting generated prose.
1652: 
1653: It remains to prove that the displayed obstruction list is complete.  Suppose an
1654: accepted claim fails the publication-safety interface.  Choose an inclusion-minimal
1655: subrecord of its acceptance entry that still witnesses failure.  The certificate
1656: schema has exactly seven coordinates, and the first two of them are not extra
1657: obstruction families.  The claim-kind coordinate $K$ only determines the finite
1658: gate set $G(c)$ through $\Gamma$; once $K$ lies in the seven-kind domain, any
1659: failure caused by that determination is a missing assigned gate and hence type~2.
1660: The evidence-level coordinate $\ell$ only chooses between the path-verified and
1661: command-run interpretations of the boundary field; its failures therefore appear
1662: either as absent evidence at the declared level, type~1, or as absent command-run
1663: coordinates, type~5.  Thus every possible failure must be witnessed in one of
1664: the five substantive schema fields $E,R,H,D,B$.
1665: 
1666: If the failing field is the evidence surface $E$, the claim is not tied to a
1667: submitted path, bundle path, manifest row, log path, pinned commit, or explicit
1668: historical boundary at the declared level.  The minimal subrecord is therefore
1669: type~1.  If the failing field is the gate-record field $R$, some gate in the
1670: deterministic finite set $G(c)$ has no pass record, no decision, or no evidence
1671: pointer lying in the submitted surface; this is type~2.  If the failing field is
1672: the human-promotion field $H$, then all non-route evidence may be present but the
1673: claim has crossed from intake or source material into active publication work
1674: without the required human promotion marker; this is type~3.  If the failing
1675: field is the discovery field $D$, then a discovery or classifier-shift reading is
1676: being inferred from statement-only, code-only, mechanical, compression-only, or
1677: classifier-scored material without the pinned certificate, ledger, lineage, and
1678: boundary rows; this is type~4.  Finally, if the failing field is the boundary
1679: field $B$, the record either raises path-verified evidence to command-run
1680: strength without command, source commit, environment, exit code, and log path, or
1681: omits the explicit no-command-run boundary; this is type~5.
1682: 
1683: The preceding paragraph exhausts the schema fields: $K$ and $\ell$ reduce to the
1684: same five substantive checks, and $E,R,H,D,B$ are precisely the five displayed
1685: obstruction types.  Minimality removes all unread fields and leaves exactly one
1686: of the five obstruction skeletons.  Conversely, if no bad subrecord of type~1--5
1687: is present, then each schema check in
1688: Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier} succeeds: the evidence
1689: surface is present at the declared level, every required gate has its recorded
1690: pass, the route-boundary field is correct, discovery rows are present exactly
1691: when required, and command-run strength is not asserted without command-run
1692: fields.  Hence the verifier accepts the certificate and the publication-safety
1693: interface holds for $c$ at the declared evidence level.
1694: 
1695: The final sentence follows from the construction.  The verifier reads only the
1696: finite certificate fields and the finite dispatch table, so the theorem
1697: classifies certificate failures for review.  It does not assert a lower bound,
1698: complexity separation, automated theorem-proving method, or fresh run of any
1699: external command.\end{proof}
1700: 
1701: \begin{theorem}[Finite extension-budget normal form]
1702: \label{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}
1703: Fix the dispatch table $\Gamma$, the finite certificate schema, and an accepted
1704: finite certificate
1705: \(\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\) for a load-bearing package claim $c$.
1706: Let $c^{+}$ be a proposed stronger reading of the same row which is not
1707: record-equivalent to $c$ in the sense of
1708: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}.  Then $c^{+}$ is
1709: accepted by the publication-safety interface if and only if the submitted finite
1710: record surface contains an extension record in the following normal form:
1711: \[
1712: \Delta(c,c^{+})=(\Delta K,\Delta E,\Delta H,\Delta R,\Delta D,\Delta B),
1713: \]
1714: where the nonempty coordinates are exactly the coordinates needed by the stronger
1715: reading:
1716: \begin{enumerate}
1717: \item $\Delta K$ names every additional claim-kind component asserted by
1718: $c^{+}$;
1719: \item $\Delta E$ supplies the submitted evidence surfaces for those added
1720: components at the declared evidence level;
1721: \item $\Delta R$ supplies pass records for every gate in
1722: \(\bigcup_{k\in K\cup\Delta K}\Gamma(k)\) not already supplied by $R$;
1723: \item $\Delta H$ supplies any newly asserted promotion, route, venue, or external
1724: instantiation boundary;
1725: \item $\Delta D$ supplies the pinned discovery-source rows whenever the stronger
1726: reading adds discovery, classifier-shift, or theorem-content-delta content; and
1727: \item $\Delta B$ supplies the command, source-state, environment, exit-code, and
1728: log-path tuple whenever the stronger reading raises a path-verified row to
1729: command-run strength.
1730: \end{enumerate}
1731: Equivalently, every genuine strengthening has positive finite extension budget:
1732: at least one coordinate of \(\Delta(c,c^{+})\) must be nonempty, and every
1733: nonempty obstruction class from
1734: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} must be paid by the
1735: corresponding coordinate of \(\Delta(c,c^{+})\).  Without this extension record,
1736: the stronger reading has an inclusion-minimal bad subrecord and remains outside
1737: the certificate constructed in
1738: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}.
1739: \end{theorem}
1740: 
1741: \begin{proof}
1742: First suppose that the extension record is present.  Enlarge the certificate by
1743: replacing its fields with
1744: \[
1745: (K\cup\Delta K,\ell,E\cup\Delta E,H\cup\Delta H,
1746: R\cup\Delta R,D\cup\Delta D,B\cup\Delta B),
1747: \]
1748: where the unions mean finite row union with the original row identifiers retained.
1749: The six clauses of Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table} are
1750: then exactly the six requirements displayed in the statement.  Clause~A1 reads
1751: the enlarged kind set and evidence level.  Clause~A2 reads the enlarged evidence
1752: surface.  Clause~A3 reads the enlarged gate-pass field and checks every gate
1753: assigned by $\Gamma$ to the enlarged kind set.  Clause~A4 reads the enlarged
1754: promotion and boundary field.  Clause~A5 reads the enlarged discovery-interface
1755: field exactly when discovery is present in the enlarged kind set.  Clause~A6
1756: reads the enlarged evidence-boundary field and accepts command-run strength only
1757: when the command tuple is present.  Therefore
1758: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} gives
1759: \(\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}(c^{+},\mathcal C\cup\Delta)\), and the finite interface
1760: accepts the stronger reading at precisely the declared evidence level.
1761: 
1762: Conversely, suppose $c^{+}$ is accepted.  Since $c^{+}$ is not
1763: record-equivalent to $c$, Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}
1764: implies that some finite coordinate read by the verifier differs from the
1765: coordinate of \(\mathcal C(c)\).  Collect the differences in the six displayed
1766: components: additional kinds in $\Delta K$, additional submitted surfaces in
1767: $\Delta E$, additional gate rows in $\Delta R$, additional promotion or external
1768: boundary fields in $\Delta H$, additional discovery rows in $\Delta D$, and
1769: additional evidence-boundary or command-run data in $\Delta B$.  These are finite
1770: because the verifier reads only finite records and the dispatch table has finite
1771: domain.  Acceptance of $c^{+}$ forces each changed coordinate to satisfy the
1772: corresponding clause A1--A6, so the collected difference record has the normal
1773: form stated above.
1774: 
1775: It remains only to prove positivity and obstruction completeness.  If every
1776: coordinate of \(\Delta(c,c^{+})\) were empty, then $c$ and $c^{+}$ would induce the
1777: same values on all verifier coordinates and would be record-equivalent, contrary
1778: to hypothesis.  Thus any genuine strengthening has positive finite extension
1779: budget.  If some nonempty obstruction class is not supplied by its corresponding
1780: coordinate, Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} identifies
1781: the first failed finite field as one of the five bad-subrecord types: missing
1782: evidence surface, missing gate record, missing promotion or external boundary,
1783: missing discovery rows, or unsupported command-run escalation.  Removing all
1784: unread fields leaves an inclusion-minimal bad subrecord.  Hence a stronger
1785: reading is accepted exactly when its finite extension budget is paid in submitted
1786: coordinates, and otherwise remains outside the original certificate.\end{proof}
1787: 
1788: \begin{proposition}[Gate-interface separation]
1789: \label{prop:gate-interface-separation}
1790: The promotion, overlap, submission-state, and theorem-content delta gates are
1791: separate deterministic interfaces.  None can be replaced by passage of another
1792: one when a load-bearing claim has the corresponding component.
1793: \end{proposition}
1794: 
1795: \begin{proof}
1796: Each named gate has a distinct invariant and evidence surface in the operational
1797: definition.  Promotion asks whether a candidate has crossed the human
1798: publication boundary; its evidence is the active-paper directory and pipeline
1799: metadata.  Overlap asks whether the route duplicates or conflicts with a sibling
1800: route; its evidence is route identity, overlap ledgers, submitted-sibling paths,
1801: and board state.  Submission-state asks whether the proposed publication action
1802: is compatible with submission markers, venue state, and submission-pack metadata.
1803: The theorem-content delta gate asks whether an asserted extension contains
1804: load-bearing theorem growth; its evidence is an inventory, hard-lint surface, or
1805: delta record.  These predicates are logically independent at the interface
